# PR review

This is the orchestrator this framework's discipline modules were
building toward — a coordinated use of the ones already compiled
(`code-review`, `unit-test-writer`, `e2e-testing`, `design-system`, the
stack convention reviewer matching the diff), built on whichever tool's
own subagent delegation runs this session: Claude Code's and Cursor's are
the same shape.

**This module never sets `status: approved`.** Its output is a synthesis
a human acts on, and a *draft* `release.md` for that human to approve.
See `enforce_segregation_of_duties` in `.pdlc/phase-graph.yaml` —
PCI-DSS 6.2.3.1 requires the approver be a distinct human from the
author, and an AI's review cannot hold that accountability. Don't build
around it; work with it.

## Which diff to review

Every reviewer reviews **the ticket branch against its base** — not the
working tree, not the last commit, not "the files that look relevant".
The base is `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/phase-state.json`'s `base_ref`, the commit
SHA `start-ticket` captured for this worktree:

```bash
git diff <base_ref>...HEAD
```

An open PR's own diff is the same thing. Pass every subagent that same
diff: reviewers reading different diffs produce findings that can't be
deduped, and one that picked its own scope misses the file nobody looked
at. If `base_ref` is missing, ask which base to use — never guess.

## What to dispatch, and when

Not every reviewer applies to every PR — decide from what the diff
touches; the full matrix is `.claude/skills/pr-review/references/dispatch-and-synthesis.md`:

- **`code-review`** — always.
- **The stack convention reviewer(s) for the files touched.** A repo can
  have more than one stack — a service with its own Terraform, a
  monorepo with a frontend and a BFF — so this is a per-file answer, not
  a per-repo one. Ask for it instead of guessing, via Bash:

  ```bash
  node .pdlc/bin/spine.js stacks-for --diff <base_ref>
  ```

  Dispatch every stack it names. If it names none, these files fall
  outside every declared pattern: fall back to the reviewer whose
  description fits, and record in `review-summary.md` that the stack
  scope was inferred rather than declared — that is a gap in the module,
  worth reporting. A declared pattern can also be wrong or too broad; if
  a reviewer gets named for files it plainly has nothing to say about,
  that is the same kind of gap in the other direction.
- **`unit-test-writer`** if the diff includes source changes (so real
  coverage gets checked, not just a test file's existence);
  **`e2e-testing`** only for a user-facing flow end to end;
  **`design-system`** only if the diff touches UI.
- **`security`** — whenever the diff touches authentication or
  authorization, data handling (personal or financial data, logging,
  response payloads), calls to or from an external system, secrets, or
  LLM/agent/MCP code. `code-review` triages security on every diff; this
  is the deep pass, and it applies more often than it looks — a new
  endpoint, a log line near account data, a new dependency.

## How to dispatch — parallel where independent

Each reviewer is an independent read of the same diff — dispatch them
together as parallel subagent calls, not one after another.

## Synthesizing the findings

1. **Dedupe overlapping findings**, citing both sources, then **rank by
   severity** on `code-review`'s own blocking / non-blocking split:
   correctness and security first, complexity and convention next, style
   last.
2. **Write `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/review-summary.md`** — one consolidated
   document, not N subagent transcripts: which subagents ran, which diff
   they read, what each flagged (with file:line), what is blocking versus
   advisory. Like every artifact for this ticket it lives in
   `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/`, **not the repo root**.
3. **Draft `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/release.md`** — the launch phase's one
   required exit artifact, always a *draft* (next section).
4. **Stop there.** The release manager reads both and makes the call;
   this module never recommends "approve" or "reject". Each step in
   full: `.claude/skills/pr-review/references/dispatch-and-synthesis.md`.

## Drafting release.md

`release.md` is what the `launch` gate requires, approved by the release
manager. Nothing else here writes it, so this skill does — from the
review you just synthesized, plus `plan.md`'s rollout/rollback thinking.

In repos that use spec-kitti (the client's Spec Kit fork), check first,
via Bash: `node .pdlc/bin/spine.js spec-kitti --ticket BANK-1234`. If it
finds the feature, `rollout_plan` and `rollback_plan` come from
`specs/<###-feature>/plan.md`'s deployment section, and its tasks must
all be done (`[x]`) — an unfinished one is a blocking finding in
`review_summary`. If it reports no spec-kitti feature, use `plan.md`.

Write `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/release.md` with YAML frontmatter matching the
`release.v1.json` schema shipped at `.pdlc/schemas/release.v1.json` —
`id` equal to the ticket id in `phase-state.json`, `cycle: 1`, the real
`pr_link`, a condensed `review_summary`, `open_questions` holding every
unfixed blocking finding, `status: draft`, the rest field by field in
`.claude/skills/pr-review/references/release-frontmatter.md`, which also carries the **Design QA**
line `verification` needs: for a user-facing change, confirmation that
what shipped matches the UX section of the approved `spec.md`, and a
finding when it doesn't. Then write the body: the ranked findings, and
anything else the release manager needs.

**Do not set `status: approved`, `approved_by`, `approval_evidence`, or
any entry in `approvals`.** That is the release manager's step; their
approval needs all three — `status: approved`, `approved_by: <name>` and
`approval_evidence: <link>` to where they actually approved — or the
launch gate stays blocked. `require_approval_evidence` is on for this
client, and holds even when they say it straight to you: a typed name is
not proof.

If the launch gate in `.pdlc/phase-graph.yaml` names a `quorum` strategy
rather than a single role, the same rule applies per signature: the
artifact carries an `approvals:` list, one entry per role, each with its
own evidence link, and you write none of them.

## Rollout and rollback — what makes these fields real

`rollout_plan` and `rollback_plan` are prose fields, which makes it easy
to write something that reads fine and helps nobody at 3am. These have to
be in them.

**Rollback** — no release is complete without one. Every trigger is a
condition *plus* a threshold *plus* the
source metric that produces the number (a trigger with no threshold is a
hope that somebody notices). The plan also names an owner — a person or
on-call rotation, never "the team" — a target recovery time (RTO;
unknown means `[PENDING]` and an `open_questions` entry, never a
plausible number), the mechanism and last known good version, the
post-rollback verification (health checks, trigger metric back at
baseline, what happens to data written during the bad window), the
feature-flag kill switch check (flipping it must disable the *whole* new
path — half a kill switch is a blocking finding), and the point of no
return, "none" if there isn't one but said. Full shape:
`.claude/skills/pr-review/references/rollback-plan.md`.

**Rollout**: per environment, who or what triggers it, the deploy step,
post-deploy configuration, one **concrete** verification check, a named
owner, the contact chain if a step fails, any ordering constraints. Read
the real pipeline; a generic description of how deploys work is worse
than nothing. Shape: `.claude/skills/pr-review/references/deployment-runbook.md`.

## What verification actually points at

`verification` degrades into "we'll watch the dashboard" unless it names
things. Four sources, all of which exist before this draft is written:
**the spec's observability section** (a metric it promised and the diff
doesn't emit is a blocking finding), **the three dashboard audiences**
(service, business, capacity — say which is watched, and for how long),
**the SLO and its error budget**, and **the rollback triggers**, the same
numbers read the other way round. Nobody having said what to watch is an
`open_questions` entry naming the gap, never a metric you have not
confirmed exists. All four: `.claude/skills/pr-review/references/verification-sources.md`.

## Verification is a check against named criteria

The other half of `verification` is not "what will we watch" but "did
what we already promised actually happen": a check against a **named
prior artifact's criteria, item by item**, never its own definition of
done. Name the source, then mark each criterion
met / not met / `[PENDING]`, every "met" carrying its evidence — met
with no evidence is an unchecked criterion. One unmet criterion with no
mitigation plan
**is a fail**, not a footnote: `open_questions`, and blocking in
`review_summary`. And
**never add a criterion the source artifact doesn't contain** — that gap
belongs to an earlier phase. Sources and rules in full:
`.claude/skills/pr-review/references/verification-sources.md`.

## No invention — sourced, `[PENDING]`, or `[ASSUMPTION]`

Every claim in `release.md` is sourced (a reviewer finding with its
file:line, a line from `plan.md`, a real dashboard, a named human), or
`[PENDING]`, or `[ASSUMPTION: …]`. Thresholds, recovery times and canary
percentages with none of the three are invented — and a release plan on
invented numbers fails exactly when it is needed.

## Example release.md frontmatter

Abridged; the full version is `.claude/skills/pr-review/references/worked-example.md`.

```markdown
---
id: BANK-4821
cycle: 1
title: Statement export times out for accounts with 10k+ transactions
pr_link: "https://github.com/example-bank/export-api/pull/1183"
review_summary: >
  code-review, java-conventions and unit-test-writer reviewed
  base_ref...HEAD (14 files, +412/-63). One blocking finding, since fixed
  (ExportJobController.java:88, missing ownership check). Two advisory.
rollout_plan: >
  Ships behind EXPORT_ASYNC_THRESHOLD, set above any real account size so
  the async path is dark on deploy; lowered to 10000 for the commercial
  cohort 24h later, then fleet-wide once queue backlog stays flat.
rollback_plan: >
  Triggers: export error rate above 1% over 15 min (statement-export
  dashboard), or job-queue depth above its alert threshold for 15 min
  (job-queue metrics). Mechanism: raise EXPORT_ASYNC_THRESHOLD past any
  real account size — config only, no deploy. Owner: export-api on-call.
  Target RTO: 10 minutes. Kill switch verified, no point of no return.
  Post-rollback: error rate back at baseline, no jobs queued.
verification: >
  Export p99 latency and export-timeout error rate on the existing
  statement-export dashboard, watched 72h after each threshold change.
  Design QA: no user-facing surface changed, per spec.md's UX section.
source_docs: ["https://client-docs.example.com/prd/statement-export-v2"]
status: draft
---
```

## What this explicitly does not do

Doesn't set `status: approved` on `release.md` or any artifact — see
above, this is not optional. Doesn't block anything itself: `release.md`
is a required `exit_artifacts` entry, so the launch gate blocks until the
release manager approves it, but this skill produces the draft, not the
blocking. `review-summary.md` is informational only.

## Failure modes

| Situation | Correct behaviour |
|---|---|
| Rollback thresholds or an RTO are unknown | `[PENDING]` plus an `open_questions` entry. Never invent a number. |
| The change ships behind a flag with no kill switch, or one that only covers part of it | Raise it as a blocking finding in `review_summary`. Don't quietly omit it. |
| A user-facing change and no Design QA check against the approved `spec.md` | Add it to `verification`; a mismatch with the approved UX is a finding. |
| The user asks you to approve or merge | Out of scope. Hand over the synthesis and the draft; the approval is a named human's, recorded with evidence. |
| A reviewer's finding is still unfixed | It stays in `open_questions` and in `review_summary` as blocking. Never paper over it to close the draft. |
